<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cheyenne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tsitsistas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cheyenne are an Algonquian-speaking Indigenous people of the Great Plains, who moved west from the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> region and took up a horse-based bison-hunting life in the 18th century. Allied with the Lakota, they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the wars of the 1860s and 1870s, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-little-bighorn">Battle of the Little Bighorn</a> in 1876. Today they are divided into the Northern Cheyenne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> and the Southern Cheyenne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cheyenne.jpg" alt="Cheyenne" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilcrease_-_Cheyenne_Beaded_Dress.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Cheyenne are made up of two tribes, the Tsitsistas and the Suhtai, which merged in the early 19th century; the Suhtai, whose speech and customs differed slightly, travelled with the Tsitsistas and joined them after 1832. The name Cheyenne comes from a Lakota word, Šahíyena, 'little Šahíya', whose meaning is uncertain. Their earliest written record dates from the mid-17th century, when a group visited the French Fort Crevecoeur, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoria">Peoria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. They then lived between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> and Mille Lacs Lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a>, collecting wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and hunting bison. Tribal history says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assiniboine">Assiniboine</a> drove them to Minnesota and North Dakota, where they built villages such as Biesterfeldt on the Sheyenne River. They first reached the Missouri River in 1676.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Pushed west by the Lakota, the Cheyenne formally allied with the Arapaho around 1811, and the alliance helped them expand their territory over much of the central Plains. By 1820 traders reported them at present-day Denver and on the Arkansas River. After Bent's Fort was built, a large part of the tribe moved south and became the Southern Cheyenne, while the rest stayed on the North Platte and Yellowstone headwaters as the Northern Cheyenne. Emigrant traffic along the trails from the 1840s increased competition for water and game, and cholera in 1849 killed about 2,000 Cheyenne, one-half to two-thirds of their population.</p><p>The Cheyenne fought the United States Army for over two decades from 1856. With the Arapaho and Lakota they fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-clouds-war">Red Cloud's War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-sioux-war-of-1876">Great Sioux War of 1876</a>, and a peaceful encampment of mostly women, children and elderly was massacred by US soldiers at Sand Creek. A reservation on the Tongue River, now the Northern Cheyenne Indian Reservation, was created by executive order on 16 November 1884 and extended in 1900. Along with the Lakota and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a>, the Cheyenne were among the last nations forced onto reservations.</p><p>Today the Cheyenne are two federally recognised nations: the Northern Cheyenne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> and the Southern Cheyenne, who form the Cheyenne and Arapaho Tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> with the Southern Arapaho. Historical figures include Black Kettle, Dull Knife, Little Wolf, Roman Nose and Two Moons, and the historian George Bent. The Southern Cheyenne artist Oakerhater is a canonised saint of the Episcopal Church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Cheyenne were a horse and warrior people. A warrior was also a protector, provider and leader, and men gained rank by counting coups, acts of bravery in battle. Their government was centred on the Arrow Keeper and the Council of Forty-Four, with four delegates from each of ten principal bands; the council also regulated the military societies. Women tanned hides and made lodges and clothing, and gathered roots and berries. The Cheyenne language belongs to the Algonquian family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cheyenne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 18th century the Lakota obtained horses from the <b>Cheyenne</b> along the Missouri River, calling them šuŋkawakaŋ, “dog of power”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Lakota, Northern <b>Cheyenne</b> and Arapaho warriors killed Lieutenant Colonel George Custer and some 268 soldiers in Montana Territory.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-25">25 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The 7th Cavalry under George Armstrong Custer attacked a <b>Cheyenne</b> village on the Washita River, killing Black Kettle and many women and children.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-27">27 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After gold was found in the Black Hills in 1874, the Great Sioux War followed; Lakota and <b>Cheyenne</b> under Sitting Bull and Crazy Horse defeated Custer at the Little Bighorn in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-sioux-nation">Great Sioux Nation</a></li></ul></section>
